      simpleExplanation: "If you can rent out a house and earn rent while keeping ownership, why can't you 'rent out' money the same way? The author explains that a house survives being used, so the owner can retain it and just collect rent - but money gets consumed/transformed the moment it's used (spent or invested), so its owner can't keep 'renting' it out; the only way to earn from money is to actually put it at risk in a real business or exchange it for something real, sharing in the resulting profit or loss.",
      academicExplanation: "The objection here treats money as a commodity that may be sold, bought and rented like any conventional asset - a fixed charge for the use of money, irrespective of how the borrower actually uses it. The author's response (linking to broader discussion of capital as a factor of production) is that capital items that can be leased must be nonconsumable, with their corpus surviving use so the lessor can retain ownership while the lessee benefits from usufruct (e.g. buildings, machinery). Money (gold, silver historically, or paper currency today) is instead 'consumed' or transformed with its use - its corpus does not survive as a distinct owned asset while someone else benefits from it - so monetary units cannot be leased; the risk of loss attaches to the money capital itself, and its form changes altogether upon use. One can only derive benefit from money by giving it up in exchange for commodities or services under a valid sale or lease structure. Real sector business transactions take the form of cash/credit sale, loaning, or leasing, each with different implications for ownership/risk/liability transfer; a lender who lends money bears no risk and has an unconditional right to recovery regardless of the borrower's profit or loss - hence cannot claim any return (interest). If a fund provider wants a profit on money, they must agree to bear the risk of loss, in which case profit is distributed per a pre-agreed ratio while loss is shared per investment proportion (with the fund user's own loss being unrewarded labour). Only if money is transformed into a fixed/nonconsumable asset does the owner gain the right to lease it on rent - and only then while bearing ownership-related risks/expenses.",
