      overview: "Critics argue lenders should be able to index loans to inflation to avoid losing purchasing power; the author responds this would still constitute Riba, since a loan is a noncommutative contract that must be repaid in the same kind and quantity regardless of value changes.",
      simpleExplanation: "Some argue that if inflation erodes the value of money, a lender should be allowed to demand a bit more back to make up for the loss - otherwise they're worse off for having lent the money at all. The author responds that a loan in Islam is meant to be a favor, not a business deal, so it must be repaid in exactly the same amount lent, no matter what happens to prices in between - if you want protection against inflation, don't lend money as a loan; instead put it into a real business or asset and share in the actual profit or loss.",
      academicExplanation: "The inflation-indexation argument holds that borrowers should indemnify lenders for the loan amount's loss of purchasing power due to inflation, and criticizes Islamic finance theory for rejecting indexation of financial liabilities to gold, goods baskets or stable currencies. The author responds that interest cannot be legalized on the inflation basis because loaning in Islam is a noncommutative, virtuous activity, not a profit-seeking business transaction; profit can be earned only if capital is linked to liability, risk or responsibility, and Islamic finance has no provision for linking a debt/receivable to any currency or commodity value. The clear injunctions of the Qur'an and Sunnah require that a loan/debt be repaid exactly in the same kind and quantity, regardless of any change in the value of the currency or commodity lent - citing Qur'an 2:279 as too clear a Nass (text) for Ijtihad (independent legal reasoning) to override. During inflation, money's intrinsic characteristics (medium of exchange, unit of account) remain intact; only its relative future exchange value changes - something that has occurred even with full-bodied coins since money's introduction (e.g. silver dirhams depreciating against gold dinars even during the early Caliphate), yet no reference to indexation exists anywhere in the historical Islamic jurisprudence literature. Renowned jurists like Al-Kasani and Ibn Qudama held that a borrower must repay the same coins/currency taken, regardless of value change. Drawing an inflation-adjustment benefit from a loan (an inherently nonremunerative, noncommutative contract) violates the Shari'ah - money kept by the lender in their own coffer would also lose value, so indexing the loan amount draws an unjustified benefit purely from having lent it. Even conventional finance doesn't typically use indexation for this purpose, instead applying floating rates prospectively (affecting only the remaining period, not accrued liability) - and Islamic banks are similarly allowed floating/variable rates in modes like leasing, but this cannot retroactively affect debt liability once created (e.g. rental for a period, once accrued, cannot be indexed after the fact).",
