      title: "Time Value of Money and Islamic Banking",
      overview: "Two opposing misconceptions exist - that Islamic banks improperly charge time value of money, or that rejecting interest means rejecting time value entirely; the author clarifies time value is accepted in pricing goods/usufruct via trade, but never in pricing a loan or debt itself.",
      simpleExplanation: "Two groups get this wrong in opposite directions: one says 'if Islamic banks charge more for credit sales than cash sales, that's basically interest'; the other says 'if Islamic finance truly rejects interest, it must also reject any idea that time affects value, so credit prices should equal cash prices.' The author says both are wrong - time genuinely affects the price of real goods and services (a valid business consideration), but it can never be used to price a pure loan or debt itself; once a price is agreed in a sale, it's locked in and can't be increased just because payment is late.",
      academicExplanation: "Two opposing misconceptions are addressed: (1) that Islamic banks charging higher credit prices than cash/spot prices are not really Islamic, or (2) that avoiding interest necessarily means negating time value of money entirely, requiring identical cash and credit prices. The author (referencing Chapter 4 in detail) explains that Shari'ah approves time value in the trading/exchange of real goods, but not in Qard (loan) or Dayn (debt), against which one cannot derive benefit. Pricing of goods/usufruct is a major part of any business transaction, and place/time are legitimate pricing factors (a commodity may be cheaper in one market/season than another) - Shari'ah recognizes value differences due to place and time and does not prohibit realizing time value of money in a genuine exchange-based (sale/leasing) transaction. What is prohibited is any claim to time value as a predetermined, rate-based quantity unrelated to real business - i.e. charging extra purely because payment is delayed, on an already-fixed price. There is near-consensus among scholars that a credit price can genuinely exceed a cash price, provided one single price is agreed at contract execution (detailed in Chapter 6, Sections 6.5.3 and 6.8); similarly, in Salam, the future delivery price is naturally less than the eventual delivery-date price - itself tantamount to accepting time value in pricing. What is prohibited is any addition to a price already agreed, due to delayed payment - because a sold (credit) commodity generates a debt belonging permanently to the buyer, and the seller loses the right to re-price something they no longer own. This connects to the prohibition of Riba Al-Fadl (deferred exchange of gold, silver and similar commodities used as media of exchange), which bans deferred exchange of homogeneous monetary-type items except hand-to-hand and equal-for-equal - implying Shari'ah prohibits mutual exchange of gold/silver/monetary values except when simultaneous, since one party could otherwise benefit from a received medium of exchange without giving its counter-value. The author distinguishes valuation of a credit period for goods/usufruct pricing from the conventional 'opportunity cost'/'time value' concept: 'mark-up' in trade is permissible (subject to sale-of-goods Shari'ah rules), but interest (an increase over a loan/debt) is prohibited - no time value can be added to a loan/debt principal after it is created or the buyer's liability fixed. There is no justification for assuming a zero rate of time preference in an Islamic economy; economic agents can have positive time preferences reflected in market-determined pricing indicators (demand/supply), but one cannot add value to loans/debts specifically on account of time.",
      examExplanation: "Time value IS accepted: in pricing goods/usufruct via genuine sale/lease transactions (credit price > cash price is fine if fixed once at execution); in Salam (future delivery price < spot price). Time value is NOT accepted: as a basis for pricing a loan/debt itself, or for adding to an already-fixed sale price due to late payment. Connects to Riba Al-Fadl's ban on deferred exchange of gold/silver/monetary items.",
