      relatedConcepts: ["Musharakah", "Mudarabah", "Murabaha", "Ijarah", "Salam", "Shirkah"],
      examRelevance: "core",
      difficulty: "advanced",
      issue: "Should Islamic banks avoid all debt-creating modes (Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam) and rely exclusively on profit/loss-sharing (Musharakah/Mudarabah) as the only genuine alternative to interest?",
      criticism: "A number of economists and financial experts argue Musharakah and Mudarabah are the only modes that can genuinely serve as an alternative to interest; Islamic banks should either operate on a return-free basis or exclusively via PLS, meaning debt should not really be part of Islamic banking and finance.",
      authorsResponse: "This conflates two distinct questions: the permissibility of debt-creating modes (settled - the Prophet himself borrowed and incurred debt for personal and State needs, and Murabaha/Salam/Ijarah remain legitimate operating tools provided debt carries no charge over its principal) and the preference for equity-based modes over debt-creating ones (a genuine but separate policy question). Fixity of profit margin is not itself a problem; what matters is whether genuine exchange of commodities/services/labour occurs under the relevant Shari'ah rules - improper application can make even Musharakah or Mudarabah non-compliant, so the issue is proper execution, not which mode is chosen. Suitability also depends on ground realities and depositor risk profile: banks holding risk-averse depositors' (e.g. a pensioner's or widow's) funds as a trust should invest them in trade/Ijarah-based modes rather than Shirkah-based ventures.",
      studentTakeaway: "When evaluating a debt-creating-mode transaction, ask two separate questions: is it permissible (usually yes, if properly structured) and is it preferable to an equity-based alternative given the depositor's risk profile and the client's actual business need (sometimes no) - don't collapse these into a single yes/no judgment.",
