      criticism: "Two objections: (1) banking means intermediating between savers and borrowers and dealing in documents only; Islamic banks getting involved in real trading/direct business exposes them and investors to unnecessary risk; (2) IFIs charge market-related rates and should instead give return-free financing, functioning as social security/charity institutions.",
      authorsResponse: "On the first objection: real-sector merchant/universal banking is already common conventional practice (Japan, Germany, Switzerland, Netherlands); the universal/investment banking model actually suits Islamic banks better than pure intermediation, since it helps address problems like information asymmetry and allows passing higher profits to depositors, with risk manageable via Islamic finance's own risk-mitigation tools and regulatory firewalls. On the second: profit-earning is not the issue - how it is earned is; giving return-free loans systematically is not viable per basic demand/supply logic, and business/benevolence are two separate things - a bank holding depositors' funds under a specific mandate has no right to dishonour that mandate by acting as a charity, though it may separately engage in social welfare activities subject to board policy.",
