      sectionTitle: "Divergence between Theory and Practice",
      title: "Divergence between Theory and Practice",
      overview: "Critics note the pioneers of Islamic finance envisioned PLS modes as central, yet Murabaha and Ijarah now dominate practice - the 'Murabaha Syndrome'; the author frames this as evolution of the concept rather than betrayal of it, while acknowledging the underlying pattern.",
      simpleExplanation: "Early Islamic finance thinkers expected profit-and-loss-sharing partnerships to be the main tool, barely even mentioning Murabaha - but today, most Islamic banks rely heavily on Murabaha (nicknamed the 'Murabaha Syndrome') and PLS is a small fraction of business. The author admits this is a real pattern but argues it reflects the field learning and adapting over time - not a betrayal of the original vision - since trading is a genuinely accepted Islamic activity, and most depositors (pensioners, widows) need safer investments that PLS modes can't always provide.",
      academicExplanation: "Critics observe Islamic banks have deviated substantially from their philosophical basis, with the concept changing visibly since the 1940s-1980s evolution period, when PLS modes were envisioned as THE alternative to interest for rectifying socio-economic injustice. Neither Dr Nejatullah Siddiqi's 'Banking without Interest' nor Dr Uzair's 'Interest-free Banking' even reference Murabaha; Pakistan's pioneering Council of Islamic Ideology Report (1980) allowed Murabaha only hesitantly, limited to unavoidable transformation-process cases. Shaikh Muhammad Taqi Usmani himself wrote that Shari'ah scholars allowed Murabaha/Ijarah only where Musharakah cannot work, with certain conditions - not as a permanent rule for all transactions or the entirety of Islamic banks' operations. Yet practically, Murabaha and Ijarah dominate heavily while PLS use is negligible, even in institutions where Shaikh Usmani himself serves as Shari'ah supervisor - a phenomenon dubbed the 'Murabaha Syndrome' by Yousef (2005), carrying an ironic undertone about IFIs' actual operations. The author's response reframes this divergence as evolution rather than betrayal: Islamic finance is still evolving on its basic philosophy/principles, and this divergence 'refers more to evolution of the concept... than to its divergence.' Trading is an accepted Islamic activity, and if business ethics/prohibitions identified in Hadith/jurisprudence are observed, it facilitates human welfare, wealth growth and broader distribution of bounties; in many cases, trade or leasing are simply the only realistic options. Crucially, a vast majority of depositors (especially in developing countries) are low-income people - pensioners, widows, lower-middle-class groups - whose money should not be invested in risky PLS ventures. However, the author does concede a genuine prescription: in single-transaction contexts or where satisfactory documentation is available, Islamic banks SHOULD use Musharakah, since it yields higher returns - implying the current heavy Murabaha reliance is not entirely justified even by risk-profile logic alone.",
      examExplanation: "'Murabaha Syndrome' (Yousef, 2005) = the ironic gap between PLS-centric founding theory and Murabaha/Ijarah-dominated practice. Founding texts (Siddiqi's 'Banking without Interest', Uzair's 'Interest-free Banking') didn't even mention Murabaha; Pakistan's 1980 CII Report allowed it only hesitantly. Author's framing: 'evolution of the concept' rather than 'divergence' from it - but he still concedes banks SHOULD use Musharakah more where documentation/risk conditions allow.",
