      criticism: "Income earned from a prohibited source (interest) should not be used to fund a business based on Shari'ah principles - using interest income as seed capital for an Islamic window or branch is seen as tainting the new institution's foundation.",
      authorsResponse: "The argument is ill-founded: someone intending to stop a prohibited practice should be encouraged, and a start-up transition period is needed. Qur'an 2:275 and 2:278 establish that past interest-based gains may be kept (with accountability left to the Hereafter) upon future cessation, and repentance entitles recovery of principal - giving a clear line of action allowing prior interest income to become seed capital for an Islamic institution, provided its operations are Shari'ah-compliant and totally segregated from ongoing interest-based business going forward. In many cases, the whole bank's capital would not even be interest income to begin with.",
      alternativeView: "A related, stronger objection asks whether such institutions should altogether abandon interest-based business and transform their entire operations to Shari'ah-compliant ones, per the Qur'anic direction. The author agrees this is the ideal requirement and should be a defined-period policy target (especially for Muslim-majority-country institutions), while conceding a mega multinational conventional institution cannot realistically transform overnight and should instead be encouraged to launch Shari'ah-compliant business at whatever scale it can afford.",
