      title: "Imposing Penalties on Defaulters",
      overview: "Critics argue penalizing defaulters contradicts the Qur'an's call for leniency toward struggling debtors; the author distinguishes wilful defaulters (who may be penalized, with proceeds to charity) from genuinely struggling debtors (who must be given relief), and separates Qard (loan) from Dayn (business-created debt) for this purpose.",
      simpleExplanation: "The Qur'an tells creditors to be patient with debtors in genuine difficulty, even to forgive debt - so how can Islamic banks charge late-payment penalties? The author responds that most late-paying clients are actually resourceful businesspeople deliberately avoiding payment while living well, not genuinely struggling debtors - and Islamic law treats a simple personal loan (where leniency is required) differently from a debt created by a real business deal like a sale or lease (where a late-payment fine, going to charity rather than the bank's profit, is allowed to deter deliberate non-payment).",
      academicExplanation: "Critics note the Qur'an recommends giving debtors in difficulty more time, or even forgiving the debt entirely, yet IFIs impose percentage-based annual penalties on late payment - making their effective financing cost equal to or higher than conventional banks, when they should instead give extra time without extra charge. The author acknowledges default is a major global financial-industry challenge; the conventional system has a built-in control tool (interest charged to defaulters becomes part of the conventional institution's income), while Shari'ah scholars have allowed IFIs to charge penalties specifically to DISCIPLINE defaulters, with the crucial condition that penalty proceeds must go to CHARITY, never becoming part of the bank's own income. The situation, the author argues, 'is not so simple': most bank clients on the assets side are resourceful businesspeople who withhold payment while continuing luxurious lifestyles - such wilful defaulters do NOT fall under the Qur'anic category of 'debtors in difficulty' entitled to relaxation/waiver. While full, timely debt repayment cannot be overemphasized in Islamic economics, a crucial distinction must be drawn between WILFUL default and ACTUAL default arising from genuine economic hardship; per Shari'ah rules, wilful defaulters are treated like usurpers, required to return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property itself. A further key distinction separates Qard (a pure loan) from Dayn (a debt created through a business transaction like sale or Ijarah): jurists approve penalty imposition only in the Dayn case; for a pure Qard, the creditor should give more time as the Qur'an directs, but if a Dayn-based liability payment is delayed through negligent tactics, the client can be fined (fine going to charity) and even required to compensate the bank for actual loss. The OIC Fiqh Council has resolved that penalty provisions become null and void when a client proves their failure was due to a reason beyond their control, or proves the bank incurred no actual loss from the breach. The author frames widespread default as a 'socio-economic evil of the present age', largely attributable to the unjust principles of the capitalist/interest-based system and legal loopholes; Islamic banks, working within the same overall environment, are permitted penalties precisely because default hits them harder than conventional institutions (they cannot claim 'cost of funds' or liquidated damages the way conventional banks can), and because default ultimately harms deposit holders - making every possible step to minimize default necessary.",
