  chapterSummary: "Chapter 17 systematically catalogues and appraises the most common conceptual and practical criticisms of Islamic banking and finance, from the meaning of Riba and time value of money to benchmarking, collateral, penalties and combined contracts. The author's method throughout is to distinguish genuine Shari'ah violations from structural misconceptions, separate permissibility from preference, and - notably - concede real, unresolved gaps (the 'Murabaha Syndrome', the need for state support to realize socio-economic goals) rather than dismissing every objection. The chapter models how to evaluate Islamic finance critically: taking both the harshest external criticism and the movement's own internal self-scrutiny seriously.",
